(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["anatomy-exam-2-anatomy-master-exam-form-c"]={"title":"Anatomy Comprehensive Master Exam — Form C","category":"Anatomy","questions":[{"q":"How does the skin achieve heat regulation, per the material?","choices":["Only through variation in stratum corneum thickness","Through sweat glands, blood vessels, and fat deposits","Only through variation in melanin production levels","Only through changes in nail bed blood flow rate"],"answer":1,"exp":"The skin achieves heat regulation through sweat glands, blood vessels, and fat deposits. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"How does the skin provide sensation, and what example is given?","choices":["By way of superficial nerves and their sensory endings, e.g. pain","By way of arrector pili muscle contraction, e.g. touch","By way of melanocytes detecting ultraviolet light","By way of the nail bed's blood vessels and their sensation only"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin provides sensation by way of superficial nerves and their sensory endings, with pain given as an example. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What does the skin synthesize and store, per its listed functions?","choices":["Vitamin D","Insulin","Melatonin","Vitamin C"],"answer":0,"exp":"The skin synthesizes and stores vitamin D. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What happens to melanin after it is transferred into keratinocytes?","choices":["It is eventually destroyed by lysosomes","It is transported back into melanocytes","It permanently remains unchanged for the life of the cell","It converts into keratin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Melanin, after being produced in melanosomes and transferred into keratinocytes, is eventually destroyed by lysosomes. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"What structures connect keratinocytes to each other within the stratum spinosum?","choices":["Hemidesmosomes to the basal lamina only","Melanosomes","Keratohyalin granules","Tonofibrils"],"answer":3,"exp":"Keratinocytes within the stratum spinosum are connected to other keratinocytes by tonofibrils. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"How does the skin provide protection, per the material?","choices":["Only by regulating body temperature via evaporative cooling mechanisms","Only through sensory nerve endings that detect pressure and touch","Protects the body from environmental effects, such as abrasions and harmful substances","Only by producing vitamin D upon direct sun exposure to the epidermis and dermis"],"answer":2,"exp":"The skin's protection function guards the body from environmental effects, such as abrasions and harmful substances. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"What is the cutaneous plexus, and where is it located?","choices":["A network of nerves found in the epidermis, transmitting temperature signals to the spinal cord","A network of sweat gland ducts draining directly into the papillary dermis","A lymphatic network found only in the dermis, unrelated to blood supply","An interconnected network of blood vessels found in the subcutaneous layer, just below the reticular layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"The cutaneous plexus is an interconnected network of blood vessels found in the subcutaneous layer, just below the reticular layer of the dermis; its branches supply blood to the lipid layer as well as the skin. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"What is the papillary plexus?","choices":["A lymphatic vessel network in the hypodermis, draining fluid from the papillary dermis","A network of nerves that reach the stratum granulosum","Smaller branches of the cutaneous plexus that reach the epidermis","The main network of lymphatic vessels in the reticular layer"],"answer":2,"exp":"The papillary plexus consists of smaller branches from the dermal blood supply that reach the epidermis (supplying the papillary layer near the epidermal-dermal junction). — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How does regulated skin blood supply contribute to thermoregulation?","choices":["Skin blood flow only decreases when body temperature increases, conserving heat regardless of the body's actual cooling needs","Increased skin blood flow always occurs alongside increased blood flow to all other organs simultaneously, regardless of total volume","When body temperature increases, increased skin circulation permits heat loss, and vice versa; increased blood flow to skin results in decreased blood flow to other organs","Skin blood flow has no relationship to body temperature regulation, remaining essentially constant across all environmental conditions"],"answer":2,"exp":"When body temperature increases, increased skin circulation permits heat loss (and vice versa when temperature decreases); increased blood flow to the skin results in decreased blood flow to other organs, as these organs interact to maintain constant total body blood flow. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What are the three general functions of the skin's nerve supply?","choices":["Only produce keratin, hardening the epidermal surface for protection","Only regulate hair growth, via direct innervation of the hair matrix","Only produce melanin, in response to sympathetic nerve stimulation","Control blood flow, adjust gland secretion, and monitor sensory receptors"],"answer":3,"exp":"The skin's nerve supply functions to control blood flow, adjust gland secretion, and monitor sensory receptors. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What do tactile corpuscles detect, and where are they located?","choices":["Light touch; located in dermal papillae and the root hair plexus","Deep pressure and vibration; located in the papillary layer","Stretch; located in the reticular layer, anchored to collagen bundles","Temperature; located in the epidermis only, near the stratum granulosum"],"answer":0,"exp":"Tactile corpuscles detect light touch and are located in the dermal papillae and root hair plexus. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What do Ruffini corpuscles detect, and where are they located within the skin?","choices":["Pain; located in the epidermis","Light touch; located in dermal papillae","Deep pressure and vibration; located in the reticular layer","Stretch; located in the reticular layer"],"answer":3,"exp":"Ruffini corpuscles detect stretch and are located in the reticular layer. — The Integumentary System - Shahsv.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"What are the glands of Zeis, and what are they associated with?","choices":["Glands that produce tears, located superiorly and laterally","Glands that produce thick secretions within the canthus","Glands that secrete a lipid-rich product along the inner eyelid margin","Sebaceous glands associated with the eyelashes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Glands of Zeis are sebaceous glands associated with the eyelashes. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"What do lacrimal caruncle glands produce, and where are they located?","choices":["Sebum; associated with the eyelashes","Thick secretions; located within the canthus areas","A lipid-rich product; located along the inner eyelid margin","Tears; located at the superior, lateral portion of the eye"],"answer":1,"exp":"Lacrimal caruncle glands produce thick secretions and are located within the canthus areas. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"An infection of which glands (other than the tarsal gland) may result in a sty?","choices":["Only the ciliary body, since it is the sole gland embedded within the eyelid margin","No gland infection produces a sty; only a tarsal gland infection produces a cyst","Glands of Zeis, lacrimal glands, or lacrimal caruncle glands (any gland other than the tarsal gland)","Only the tarsal gland, since it is the largest gland embedded within the eyelid"],"answer":2,"exp":"An infection of any of the eye's glands other than the tarsal gland (e.g., glands of Zeis, lacrimal glands, lacrimal caruncle glands) may result in a sty, whereas a tarsal gland infection specifically results in a cyst. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"How many extrinsic muscles move the eye, and how many primary actions is each associated with?","choices":["6 extrinsic muscles, each associated with one primary action","8 extrinsic muscles, each associated with no primary action","4 extrinsic muscles, each associated with two primary actions","6 extrinsic muscles, all sharing the same single primary action"],"answer":0,"exp":"There are 6 extrinsic muscles that move the eye in many directions, and each muscle is associated with one primary action. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"The cornea is described as what, in relation to the sclera?","choices":["An entirely separate tissue type unrelated to the sclera","Part of the vascular tunic, not the fibrous tunic","Modified sclera","A component of the neural tunic"],"answer":2,"exp":"The cornea is modified sclera, both belonging to the fibrous tunic (outer layer) of the eye. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Where are the photoreceptors (rods and cones) located within the retina's layered histology, relative to the choroid and vitreous chamber?","choices":["Located outside the retina entirely, within the choroid itself","Located exactly midway between the choroid and vitreous chamber","Located closest to the choroid, rather than near the vitreous chamber","Located closest to the vitreous chamber, rather than near the choroid"],"answer":2,"exp":"In the histological organization of the retina, the photoreceptors (rods and cones) are located closest to the choroid, rather than near the vitreous chamber — light must pass through the other retinal layers before reaching them. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"Into what three regions is the ear subdivided?","choices":["Anterior, middle, and posterior ear","External ear, middle ear, and inner ear","Just external and inner ear, omitting the middle","Utricle, saccule, and pinna only"],"answer":1,"exp":"The ear is subdivided into three regions: the external ear, middle ear, and inner ear. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"What structures make up the external ear?","choices":["Auricle, external acoustic meatus, tympanic membrane, and ceruminous glands","Auditory tube (pharyngotympanic tube) and tympanic cavity only","Malleus, incus, and stapes, suspended within the auricle's cartilage","Cochlea and semicircular canals, embedded within the auricle"],"answer":0,"exp":"The external ear consists of the auricle, external acoustic meatus, tympanic membrane, and ceruminous glands. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"What structures make up the middle ear?","choices":["Tympanic cavity, auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes), and auditory tube (pharyngotympanic tube)","Auricle and external acoustic meatus, along with the ceruminous glands","Cochlea, vestibule, and semicircular canals, surrounding the oval window","Only the tympanic membrane, suspended freely within the tympanic cavity"],"answer":0,"exp":"The middle ear consists of the tympanic cavity, the auditory ossicles (malleus, incus, and stapes), and the auditory tube (pharyngotympanic tube). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"What structures make up the inner ear?","choices":["Tympanic cavity and auditory ossicles only, without any membranous or fluid-filled structures","Only the cochlea, with no other structures, since the vestibule is classified separately","Auricle, external acoustic meatus, and tympanic membrane, surrounding the oval window","Receptors, membranous labyrinth (within the bony labyrinth), vestibule, semicircular canals, cochlea, utricle, and saccule"],"answer":3,"exp":"The inner ear consists of receptors, the membranous labyrinth (within the bony labyrinth), the vestibule, semicircular canals, cochlea, utricle, and saccule. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What are the three auditory ossicles, and where does the base of the stapes attach?","choices":["Only two ossicles: malleus and stapes, with the stapes base attaching at the round window","Malleus, incus, and stapes; the base of the stapes attaches at the oval window","Stapes, cochlea, and vestibule, with the stapes base attaching at the round window","Malleus, incus, and vestibule; the base attaches at the tympanic membrane"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three auditory ossicles are the malleus, incus, and stapes; the base of the stapes attaches at the oval window. — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"What does the Eustachian (auditory) tube do, and why is its horizontal positioning in children clinically relevant?","choices":["Equalizes pressure in the middle ear and allows fluid drainage; in children, its horizontal position results in stasis of fluid","Only produces earwax, unrelated to pressure regulation; its horizontal position in children affects hearing sensitivity","Connects the cochlea to the vestibule, allowing endolymph exchange; its position in children affects balance, not pressure","Has no clinical relevance regardless of positioning, since pressure equalizes passively through the tympanic membrane"],"answer":0,"exp":"The Eustachian tube equalizes pressure in the middle ear and allows fluid to drain from the middle ear; it is positioned horizontally in children, which results in stasis of fluid (a clinically relevant point, since fluid doesn't drain as efficiently). — 7. Eye and Ear Anatomy Lecture 2026.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"What four parts make up each AV valve?","choices":["Endocardium, myocardium, epicardium, and pericardium","Ring of connective tissue, cusps, chordae tendineae, and papillary muscles","Ring of connective tissue and cusps only, without any tendinous attachments","Only cusps and chordae tendineae, lacking any supporting papillary muscles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Each AV valve consists of four parts: a ring of connective tissue connecting to the heart tissue, cusps, chordae tendineae, and papillary muscles. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"What causes the AV valves to close, and what happens as a result?","choices":["Blood forces the AV valves closed, resulting in blood going through the semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic) instead, preventing backflow","The AV valves never close during the cardiac cycle, remaining open throughout systole and diastole","Papillary muscle relaxation forces the AV valves closed during ventricular systole, preventing any backflow of blood","The semilunar valves force the AV valves closed by generating backward pressure during ventricular diastole"],"answer":0,"exp":"Blood forces the AV valves closed, resulting in blood going through the semilunar valves (pulmonary and aortic) instead, preventing backflow of blood; papillary muscles contract to prevent valvular prolapse and maintain tension. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"During ventricular diastole, are the AV valves open or closed, and are the semilunar valves open or closed?","choices":["AV valves are closed; semilunar valves are open","AV valves are open; semilunar valves are closed","Both sets of valves are closed simultaneously","Both sets of valves are open simultaneously"],"answer":1,"exp":"During ventricular diastole (when the ventricles are relaxed), the AV valves are open and the semilunar valves are closed, with the chordae tendineae loose and papillary muscles relaxed. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"During ventricular systole, are the AV valves open or closed, and are the semilunar valves open or closed?","choices":["AV valves are open; semilunar valves are closed","Both sets of valves remain open throughout systole","AV valves are closed; semilunar valves are open","Neither valve set changes state during systole"],"answer":2,"exp":"During ventricular systole (when the ventricles are contracting), the AV valves are closed and the semilunar valves are open, with the chordae tendineae tense and papillary muscles contracted. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the coronary plexus, and what two autonomic components does it consist of?","choices":["The heart's autonomic innervation network, consisting of sympathetic and parasympathetic components","A structure found only within the SA node, regulating its intrinsic firing rate","A structure consisting only of sympathetic fibers, entirely lacking parasympathetic input","A network of coronary blood vessels only, unrelated to nerves or autonomic control"],"answer":0,"exp":"The heart is innervated by the autonomic nervous system, referred to as the coronary plexus, consisting of both sympathetic and parasympathetic components; this innervation can increase or decrease (but does not initiate) the heartbeat. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which nerve is the exception that only innervates the SA and AV nodes (not cardiomyocytes generally), and what is the right versus left tendency?","choices":["Sympathetic cardiac nerves; both sides innervate only the SA node exclusively","Phrenic nerve; left phrenic innervates the SA node, right phrenic innervates the AV node","Phrenic nerve; innervates only the AV node, sparing the SA node entirely","Vagus nerve (parasympathetic); right vagus tends to innervate the SA node, left vagus tends to innervate the AV node"],"answer":3,"exp":"The vagal nerve (parasympathetic) is the exception, only innervating the SA and AV nodes; the right vagus nerve tends to innervate the SA node, while the left vagus nerve tends to innervate the AV node (with significant anatomical variation). — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"What is systole, and what is diastole?","choices":["Both terms describe atrial contraction only, with ventricular activity constant throughout the cardiac cycle","Systole is contraction of the ventricles (ejecting blood into the pulmonary trunk/aorta); diastole is relaxation of the ventricles (chambers filling with blood)","Systole is relaxation of the ventricles, during which blood is ejected into the pulmonary trunk and aorta","Systole and diastole both refer to the same phase of the cardiac cycle, occurring simultaneously in atria and ventricles"],"answer":1,"exp":"Systole is contraction of the ventricles, during which blood is ejected from the ventricles into the pulmonary trunk and the ascending aorta; diastole is relaxation of the ventricles, during which the chambers fill with blood. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"What is the sinoatrial (SA) node, and where is it located?","choices":["A valve structure in the right atrium that prevents backflow of blood into the superior vena cava","A relay station in the left ventricle that amplifies electrical signals before reaching the Purkinje fibers","A node found only within the AV bundle, embedded in the interventricular septum","The cardiac pacemaker, embedded in the superior posterior wall of the right atrium"],"answer":3,"exp":"The sinoatrial (SA) node is the cardiac pacemaker, embedded in the superior posterior wall of the right atrium; it determines the rate of heart contractions. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"What are bradycardia, normocardia, and tachycardia, in terms of heart rate?","choices":["Normocardia refers to a rate above 150 bpm, while bradycardia and tachycardia both describe rates below 60 bpm","Bradycardia: slow heart rate; normocardia: normal (60-100 bpm); tachycardia: rapid heart rate","Bradycardia: rapid heart rate; tachycardia: slow heart rate, with normocardia falling in between","All three terms describe the identical heart rate, differing only in the clinical context used"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bradycardia is a slow heart rate, normocardia is a normal heart rate (60-100 bpm), and tachycardia is a rapid heart rate. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"How does the impulse from the SA node travel to the AV node?","choices":["Via the coronary sinus","Via the internodal pathways","Via the vagus nerve","Directly through the ventricular myocardium, bypassing any pathway"],"answer":1,"exp":"The impulse travels from the SA node to the atrioventricular (AV) node via the internodal pathways. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"Where is the AV node located, and how does its position compare to the SA node?","choices":["Sits in the superior posterior wall of the left atrium, identical position to the SA node","Sits in the left atrium, near the base of the mitral valve annulus","Sits within the floor or inferomedial aspect of the right atrium; it is more anterior than the SA node","Sits in the right ventricle; it is more inferior than the SA node"],"answer":2,"exp":"The AV node sits within the floor or inferomedial aspect of the right atrium; it is more anterior than the SA node. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What is the pathway of the impulse after leaving the AV node, through the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers?","choices":["AV node → AV bundle (travels along interventricular septum, divides into right and left bundle branches) → Purkinje fibers → ventricular myocardium","AV node → coronary sinus → ventricular myocardium, bypassing the bundle branches and Purkinje fibers entirely","AV bundle → AV node → Purkinje fibers → SA node, forming a closed conduction loop that circles back to the primary pacemaker","AV node → Purkinje fibers directly, bypassing the AV bundle and bundle branches, reaching the ventricles instantly"],"answer":0,"exp":"The impulse travels from the AV node to the AV bundle, which travels along the interventricular septum and then divides to form the right and left bundle branches; the bundle branches send impulses to the Purkinje fibers, which convey impulses to the ventricular myocardium. — 9. Anatomy of the Heart and Great Vessels - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What veins drain into the brachiocephalic veins?","choices":["Only the hepatic veins drain directly into this one vessel","Subclavian veins, azygos, hemi-azygos, and internal thoracic veins","Only the femoral vein drains directly into this vessel","Only the renal veins drain directly into this vessel"],"answer":1,"exp":"Subclavian veins, the azygos vein, hemi-azygos vein, and internal thoracic veins all drain into the brachiocephalic veins. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 58"},{"q":"What does the inferior vena cava collect, and what is unique about the left versus right gonadal vein drainage?","choices":["Collects most venous blood from organs inferior to the diaphragm; the right gonadal vein drains directly into the IVC, while the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein first","Collects blood only from the head and neck region; both gonadal veins drain directly into the internal jugular vein on each respective side","Both gonadal veins drain identically and directly into the IVC, without ever passing through either renal vein or the liver first","The inferior vena cava drains into the left atrium, collecting blood only from those upper body organs lying above the diaphragm and from the heart itself"],"answer":0,"exp":"The inferior vena cava collects most of the venous blood from organs inferior to the diaphragm (via common iliac veins, lumbar veins, gonadal veins, hepatic veins, and other abdominal veins); the right gonadal vein drains directly into the inferior vena cava, while the left gonadal vein drains into the left renal vein, which then drains into the inferior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What is the venous return pathway from the foot's plantar veins to the inferior vena cava?","choices":["Plantar veins → femoral vein → the inferior vena cava directly, skipping the popliteal and iliac veins entirely","Popliteal vein → plantar veins → femoral vein → external iliac vein → common iliac vein → the aorta directly, bypassing the vena cava","Plantar veins → anterior/posterior tibial and fibular veins → popliteal vein → femoral vein → external iliac vein → common iliac vein → inferior vena cava","Femoral vein → plantar veins → popliteal vein → common iliac vein → the right atrium directly, entirely bypassing the IVC"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood from the sole and digits drains to plantar veins and the ventral plantar arches, then into the anterior tibial, posterior tibial, and fibular veins, then the popliteal vein, femoral vein, external iliac vein, common iliac vein, and finally the inferior vena cava. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"What are the great and small saphenous veins, and where do they ultimately drain?","choices":["Veins that drain directly into the inferior vena cava, entirely bypassing both the femoral vein and popliteal system altogether","Superficial veins draining the dorsal venous arch of the foot, ultimately reaching the femoral vein (great saphenous) and popliteal region (small saphenous) before the deeper system","Deep veins found only within the thigh region, running alongside the femoral artery and draining directly and exclusively into the popliteal vein","Arteries supplying the foot with oxygenated blood via branches of the femoral and the popliteal arteries directly, entirely unrelated to any venous drainage"],"answer":1,"exp":"The great saphenous vein and small saphenous vein are superficial veins draining the dorsal venous arch of the foot; the great saphenous vein reaches the femoral vein (which also receives the deep femoral vein), continuing on to the external iliac vein, common iliac vein, inferior vena cava, and right atrium. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which veins drain into the internal iliac vein, and where does the median sacral vein drain instead?","choices":["Gluteal and obturator veins both drain into the external iliac vein instead; the median sacral vein drains into the right common iliac vein","All pelvic veins drain directly into the aorta itself, including the median sacral vein, with no exceptions ever occurring","Gluteal, internal pudendal, obturator, and lateral sacral veins drain into the internal iliac vein; the median sacral vein drains into the left common iliac vein","The median sacral vein drains into the internal iliac vein, exactly the same as the gluteal and obturator veins"],"answer":2,"exp":"Gluteal veins, internal pudendal veins, obturator veins, and lateral sacral veins drain into the internal iliac vein (then the common iliac vein, then the inferior vena cava); the median sacral vein is an exception, draining into the left common iliac vein instead. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"What does the anterior tibial artery form in the foot, and what does the posterior tibial artery form?","choices":["Anterior tibial artery forms the plantar arteries; posterior tibial artery instead forms the dorsalis pedis artery","Neither artery contributes to foot circulation at all, which is instead supplied entirely by the femoral artery","Both arteries together form the identical dorsalis pedis artery, with no plantar artery contribution at all","Anterior tibial artery forms the dorsalis pedis artery; posterior tibial artery forms the medial and lateral plantar arteries"],"answer":3,"exp":"The anterior tibial artery forms the dorsalis pedis artery (a common site for taking pulses), while the posterior tibial artery forms the medial and lateral plantar arteries. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 47"},{"q":"What are the two main components of blood, and what does the \"formed elements\" component include?","choices":["Plasma and serum only, with formed elements being entirely absent from whole blood","Formed elements only, with no liquid plasma component present in whole blood at all","Plasma and formed elements; formed elements include erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets","Only erythrocytes present, with no other formed elements found in whole blood"],"answer":2,"exp":"Blood consists of two components: plasma (the liquid matrix) and formed elements (blood cells and cell fragments suspended in the plasma), including erythrocytes (red blood cells), leukocytes (white blood cells), and platelets. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the functions of erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets, respectively?","choices":["All three formed elements share an identical function, each transporting oxygen and carbon dioxide equally","Platelets transport oxygen and carbon dioxide; erythrocytes are instead involved in blood clotting","Erythrocytes transport oxygen/carbon dioxide; leukocytes function in the immune system; platelets are involved in blood clotting","Erythrocytes function in immunity; leukocytes transport oxygen and carbon dioxide; platelets have no known function at all"],"answer":2,"exp":"Erythrocytes (red blood cells) transport oxygen and carbon dioxide, leukocytes (white blood cells) function in the immune system, and platelets are involved in blood clotting. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"What are the three main plasma protein types, and what percentage of plasma proteins does albumin make up?","choices":["Only two plasma protein types: albumins and globulins","Albumins (60%), globulins (35%), and fibrinogen (4%)","Hemoglobin (60%), albumins (35%), and globulins (4%)","Albumins (4%), globulins (60%), and fibrinogen (35%)"],"answer":1,"exp":"The three main plasma protein types are albumins (60%, major contributors to osmotic pressure, transport lipids/steroid hormones), globulins (35%, transport ions/hormones/lipids, immune function), and fibrinogen (4%, essential for clotting, convertible to insoluble fibrin), plus regulatory proteins (<1%). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"What percentage of body weight does whole blood approximately make up, and what is the normal blood pH range?","choices":["About 50 percent of body weight; pH 7.0 exactly","About 1 percent of body weight; pH 8.0-8.5","About 7 percent of body weight; pH 7.35-7.45","About 25 percent of body weight; pH 6.0-6.5"],"answer":2,"exp":"Whole blood makes up approximately 7 percent of body weight, with a normal pH range of 7.35-7.45. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What is the difference between hypovolemic, normovolemic, and hypervolemic?","choices":["Hypovolemic: excessive blood volume; normovolemic: low blood volume; hypervolemic: normal blood volume","These terms specifically describe blood pH levels, not blood volume states in the body","Hypovolemic: low blood volume; normovolemic: normal blood volume; hypervolemic: excessive blood volume","All three terms describe the identical blood volume state, each simply meaning normal volume"],"answer":2,"exp":"Hypovolemic describes low blood volume, normovolemic describes normal blood volume, and hypervolemic describes excessive blood volume. — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"What percentage of whole blood volume does plasma make up, and what are its three components?","choices":["About 45 percent; consists mostly of formed elements and cellular debris","About 55 percent; consists of 92% water, 7% proteins, and 1% other solutes","About 92 percent; consists almost entirely of dissolved protein alone","About 7 percent; consists almost entirely of pure water alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Plasma makes up about 55 percent of the volume of whole blood, consisting of 92 percent water, 7 percent proteins (albumin, globulins, fibrinogen, regulatory proteins), and 1 percent other solutes (electrolytes, organic nutrients, organic waste). — Anatomy - Vascular & Lymphatics - Shah Summer I UPDATEDsv-1.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"How many secondary bronchi does the right primary bronchus divide into, versus the left?","choices":["Right divides into two secondary bronchi (superior, inferior); left divides into three secondary bronchi (superior, middle, inferior)","Right divides into three secondary bronchi (one per lobe: superior, middle, inferior); left divides into two (superior, inferior)","Neither side divides into secondary bronchi; each primary bronchus enters its lung as one single, undivided tube","Both sides divide into an identical number of secondary bronchi, three each, regardless of each lung's lobe count"],"answer":1,"exp":"The right primary bronchus divides into three secondary bronchi, one for each of the right lung's three lobes (superior, middle, inferior lobar bronchus), while the left primary bronchus divides into two secondary bronchi, one for each of the left lung's two lobes (superior, inferior lobar bronchus). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"How many tertiary bronchi (and therefore bronchopulmonary segments) does each lung have?","choices":["10 tertiary bronchi and 10 bronchopulmonary segments per lung","3 tertiary bronchi, matching the 3 lobes of the right lung only","5 tertiary bronchi and 5 bronchopulmonary segments per lung","20 tertiary bronchi per lung, with no anatomical variation"],"answer":0,"exp":"The right and left lungs each have 10 tertiary bronchi and therefore 10 bronchopulmonary segments, though there is significant anatomical variation. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"Besides gas exchange, what other functions does the respiratory system serve?","choices":["Protects respiratory surfaces from dehydration, protects against pathogens, produces sound for communication, and assists in regulating blood volume/pressure/body fluid pH","Digestion of nutrients, breakdown of proteins and fats, and absorption of vitamins, functioning identically to the digestive system's role","Only gas exchange, with no other described functions beyond simply moving air into and out of the alveoli during each single breath","Only sound production for verbal communication, entirely unrelated to protection, pathogen defense, or fluid balance regulation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Beyond providing an area for gas exchange, the respiratory system protects respiratory surfaces from dehydration, provides protection against invading pathogens, produces sound involved in verbal communication, and assists in regulating blood volume, blood pressure, and body fluid pH. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 8"},{"q":"What bony and cartilaginous structures make up the nose?","choices":["The maxilla and mandible exclusively, with no cartilage or septal component involved at all","Only cartilage tissue, with no bony component at all, since the nose lacks nasal bones entirely","Only the frontal bone, with no other bony or cartilaginous components contributing to the nose's structure","Nasal bones, nasal septum (vomer and perpendicular plate of ethmoid), cartilage (external nares, alar cartilage), and nasal conchae"],"answer":3,"exp":"The nose consists of nasal bones, the nasal septum (formed by the vomer bone and perpendicular plate of the ethmoid), cartilage (including the external nares and alar cartilage forming the dorsum/bridge and apex/tip of the nose), and the nasal conchae. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"What is the key difference in apical (free) surface contact between columnar cells and pseudocolumnar cells?","choices":["Every columnar cell reaches the apical surface; not every pseudocolumnar cell reaches the apical surface","Neither cell type has any cells that ever reach the apical surface, per the material","Every pseudocolumnar cell reaches the apical surface; columnar cells largely fail to reach it","Both cell types have identical apical surface contact patterns, with every single cell reaching it"],"answer":0,"exp":"In columnar epithelium, every cell reaches the apical (free) surface, while in pseudocolumnar epithelium, not every cell reaches the apical surface (even though every cell in both types touches the basement membrane) — this is part of why pseudocolumnar epithelium falsely appears to have multiple layers. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"What is the vallecula, and where is it located relative to the epiglottis?","choices":["A cartilage ring found within the tracheal wall, inferior to the larynx","A ligament connecting the thyroid and cricoid cartilages at the front of the neck","A pocket posterior to the tongue, located superior and anterior to the epiglottis","A fold of the pharynx found only in the nasopharynx, superior to the soft palate"],"answer":2,"exp":"The vallecula is a pocket posterior to the tongue, located superior and anterior to the epiglottis. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"How many lobes does the right lung have, versus the left lung?","choices":["Right lung has two lobes (superior, inferior); left lung has three lobes (superior, middle, inferior)","Both lungs have an identical number of lobes, with three lobes each (superior, middle, inferior)","Neither lung is divided into lobes; both function as single undivided respiratory units","Right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, inferior); left lung has two lobes (superior, inferior)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The right lung has three lobes (superior, middle, and inferior), while the left lung has two lobes (superior and inferior). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What fissures does the right lung have, versus the left lung?","choices":["Both lungs have identical fissure patterns, each with one horizontal and one oblique fissure","Neither lung has any fissures; both remain completely undivided structures","Right lung has a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure; left lung has only an oblique fissure","Right lung has only an oblique fissure; left lung has both a horizontal and oblique fissure"],"answer":2,"exp":"The right lung has a horizontal fissure and an oblique fissure (separating its three lobes), while the left lung has only the oblique fissure (separating its two lobes). — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the cardiac notch, and what is the lingula?","choices":["Both structures are found identically in both lungs, each accommodating the heart's position within the thorax equally","Cardiac notch is found in the right lung only, accommodating the liver; lingula is a fold of the right lung's middle lobe","Neither structure is related to the heart's position; both instead relate to lymphatic drainage of the mediastinum","Cardiac notch is an indentation in the left lung accommodating the heart; lingula is a tongue-like structure from the superior lobe wrapping around the heart"],"answer":3,"exp":"The left lung has a cardiac notch (an indentation accommodating the heart) and a groove for the aorta; the lingula is a tongue-like structure coming out of the superior lobe that wraps around the heart. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 44"},{"q":"What is the difference between extrapulmonary and intrapulmonary bronchi?","choices":["Neither term is related to bronchial anatomy; both instead describe separate divisions of the pulmonary artery branches","Both terms describe the trachea specifically, distinguishing its cervical from its thoracic portion","Extrapulmonary bronchi are inside the lungs; intrapulmonary bronchi are outside (distinct, unrelated branching structures)","Extrapulmonary bronchi are outside the lungs; intrapulmonary bronchi are inside the lungs (same branching structures, named by location)"],"answer":3,"exp":"The primary bronchi are called extrapulmonary bronchi while outside the lungs, and intrapulmonary bronchi once inside the lungs, where they branch numerous times into secondary and tertiary bronchi. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"What is the diameter range of bronchioles, and why don't they require cartilage plates?","choices":["10-15 mm diameter; they rely mainly on cartilage rings rather than smooth muscle tone","0.3-0.5 mm diameter; they are self-supporting and therefore don't require cartilage plates","0.3-0.5 mm diameter; they require cartilage plates identical to the trachea's C-shaped rings","5-10 mm diameter; they require cartilage plates similar to the larger primary bronchi"],"answer":1,"exp":"Bronchioles have a diameter of 0.3-0.5 mm; they are self-supporting and therefore do not require cartilage plates (unlike the larger bronchi), consisting instead of smooth muscle for bronchodilation and bronchoconstriction. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 57"},{"q":"What causes bronchodilation versus bronchoconstriction of the bronchioles' smooth muscle, and what nerve senses irritants/pain there?","choices":["Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction; parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation; the phrenic nerve senses irritants and pain","The bronchioles have no sensory innervation at all; only the trachea carries pain and irritant sensory fibers","Neither autonomic division affects bronchiole smooth muscle; diameter is fixed and unresponsive to nervous input","Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation; parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction; the vagus nerve senses irritants and pain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Sympathetic stimulation causes bronchodilation, while parasympathetic stimulation causes bronchoconstriction of the bronchioles' smooth muscle; irritants and pain are sensed by the vagus nerve. — Pulmonary Anatomy - Slides .pptx, Slide 57"}]};
